## ABSTRACT
The biggest challenge of potassium-ion batteries (KIBs) application is to develop high performance electrode materials for accommodating the large-size potassium ions. Herein, by rational design, we carbonize three-dimensional (3D) order macroporous ZIF-8 to fabricate 3D interconnected nitrogen doped hierarchical porous carbon (NHPC) that shows excellent rate performance ( $94{\mathrm{mAh}}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $10.0\mathrm{Ag^{-1}}$ ), unprecedented cycle stability $\mathrm{157~mA~g^{-1}}$ after 12000 cycles at $2.0\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}};$ ) and superior reversible capacity (292 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $0.1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}^{}$ ). The 3D hierarchical porous structure diminishes the diffusion distance for both ions/electrons, while N-doping improves the reactivity and electronic conductivity via producing more defects. In addition, the bicontinuous structure possesses large specific surface area, decreasing the current density, again improving the rate performance. In-situ Raman spectra analysis confirms the potassiation and depotassiation in the N-HPC are highly reversible processes. The Galvanostatic intermittent titration (GITT) measurement and the first-principles calculations reveal that the interconnected macroporous are more beneficial to the diffusion of the $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ . This 3D interpenetrating structure demonstrates the superiority for energy storage applications.
## KEYWORDS
Ordered interconnected macropores, In-situ Raman spectra, Carbon, Nitrogen doping, Potassium-ion batteries
Lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) have been successfully applied as power sources for various electronic devices and vehicles.1 Nevertheless, lithium resources are unevenly distributed in the earth’s crust and reserves are also limited. Its applications in largescale and smart grid are restricted.2-6 It is highly desired to manufacture novel energy storage systems based on bountiful earth’s resources and low cost. Thus, it has been diffusely reported in potassium-ion batteries (KIBs) by reason that abundant potassium resources and its similar “rocking chair” working mechanism as LIBs.7-10 Additionally, the redox potential of $\mathrm{K}/\mathrm{K}^{+}$ (-2.93 V) is close to that of $\mathrm{Li/Li^{+}}$ , indicating that KIBs could achieve a high energy density due to a high voltage plateau in full cell. All these advantages of KIB make the KIB system as one of the possible alternatives to the LIB for large-scale applications.11-13 However, the major challenge in advancing KIBs system lies in finding high performance electrode materials. Due to the large ion size, it is vital to search the matching materials to accommodate the bigger $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ insertion/extraction.14 Recently, a few cathode materials (i.e. Prussian Blue $\textcircled{a}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{o}\mathbf{S}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{K}_{0.5}\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5}$ , PAN-Se)15-17 and anode materials (i.e. Bi, $\mathrm{MoSe}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ , $\mathrm{VSe}_{2}$ , Phosphorus)18-21 display decent reversible capacity for KIBs, but the cycle life for all of them cannot satisfy the need of applications in the future. Therefore, further exploration of the novel materials with superior specific capacity, extended cycle life, fine rate capability is extremely required, and may be possibly be realized by the design and synthesis of carbon-based materials.
As we all know that potassium ions could intercalate to graphite to form intercalation compounds and display a high theoretical capacity $(279\mathrm{\mA\h\g^{-1}}\cdot$ ).10, 22 But graphite as anode for KIBs always shows poor electrochemical performance for both cycle stability and rate capability due to the skeleton of graphite collapse root in the enormous volume expansion during potassiation/depotassiation.10, 23 Compared to graphitic carbon, amorphous carbon shows favorable performance in KIBs, because it has larger average inter-layer distance that could accommodate the bigger $\mathrm{K}^{\mathrm{+}}\left(2.72\mathrm{\AA}\right)$ and tolerate the volume expansion.23 Guo et al reported the amorphous carbon with ordered mesoporous as anode for KIBs, which could preserve a capacity of $146.5\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 1000 cycles at $1.0\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}.$ 23 Ji’s group demonstrated that the hard carbon microspheres show better rate property( $136\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 5C).24 Although some headway has been made in enhancing rate performance and cycling stability of the carbon materials, the current rate performance and cycling stability of amorphous carbon anode for KIBs are incomparable with high performance of LIBs.25, 26
Atomic modulation (for example, heteroatoms doping) in carbon is an efficient solution to enhance the potassium storage property because that could optimize the electronic structure of the surface of the electrode materials and enhance the electrochemistry ability.27, 28 Nitrogen (N) doping in carbon has been the most extensively investigated in LIBs and sodium-ion batteries (NIBs) systems, which could raise the reactivity and electronic conductivity via producing external defects.29-31 To promote the electrochemical property of carbon materials, another particularly effective way is to tailor porous nanostructure that can enable the active sites exposed to surface of carbon anode in the liquid electrolyte, reduced the electrons/ions transmission distance, and boost the mass transfer, thus leading to enhanced electrochemical performance.32, 33
Recently, metal organic frameworks (MOFs)-derived carbon has drawn extensive attention to electrochemical energy storage research owing to their enormous surface area, substantial microporous structure , and controllable structures.34 Nitrogen doped microporous carbon derived from a typical MOF (ZIF-8) has been used as anode for KIBs and NIBs, but it only display poor rate capacity (46 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $2.0\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}\dot{}$ ) for KIBs.35 Three-dimensional (3D) ordered MOF single crystals have generated intense interest, mainly due to their important applications in many significant fields from gas storage to biomedicine.36, 37 One can expect that 3D ordered ZIF-8 derived hierarchical N-doped carbon is able to become hopeful anode materials of KIBs for following three advantages. Firstly, periodic macropores can improve the infiltration of the liquid electrolyte in the whole electrode, alleviating the concentration polarization and enhancing rate performance and capacity of the cell. Secondly, the thickness of the wall are only nanometers, which reduces the diffusion distance of potassium ions in solidstate. It is enhancing the rate performance of the electrodes. In addition, the bicontinuous structures possess large surface area, decreasing the current density, improving the rate capability again. Thirdly, the walls and pores are bicontinuous that provides successive transport pathway through the active material (carbon) and the electrolyte phase (pores). Finally, the N-doping in carbon could also enhance the electronic conductivity and reactivity via producing additional defects.
Herein, to integrate all above mentioned methods, we designed and fabricated 3D ordered macroporous ZIF-8 single crystals derived hierarchical porous carbon with Ndoped (marked as N-HPC) that is used as anode for KIBs with unprecedented rate property (94 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $10.0\mathrm{Ag^{-1}}\$ ) and super-stable cycle performance (157 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 12000 cycles at $2.0\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}};$ . The Galvanostatic intermittent titration (GITT) measurement and the first-principles calculations jointly reveal that the interconnected microporous are more beneficial to the diffusion of the $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ .
Figure 1 shows the preparation process of N-HPC. Firstly, the highly ordered 3D polystyrene spheres (PSs) monolithic filter cakes were prepared by filtrating the monodisperse PSs solution and subsequently used as a sacrificial template to fabricate the ordered macroporous carbon (Figure S1a-b). Afterwards, the obtained PSs monolith was soaked in the ZIF-8 precursor solution and induced by $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}/\mathrm{NH}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ solution to realize the in-situ growth of ZIF-8 between the clearances existing in the neighboring PS nanoparticles. Subsequently, the 3D ordered macro-microporous ZIF8 (3DOM-ZIF-8) was obtained by removed PSs. (Figure S2a-b) Finally, the N-HPC could be successfully fabricated through the removal of PS template and heat-treated at $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in Ar atmosphere. As a contrast, the nitrogen (N)-doped porous carbon (N-PC) without using the PS template was also prepared by directly carbonization the conventional ZIF-8 (C-ZIF-8) (Figure S3).38
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and scanning electron microscope (SEM) were performed to investigate the morphology and microstructure of N-HPC sample. The N-HPC displays the polyhedral morphology structure with the size ranging from 1 to $3~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ in Figures 2a-c. The N-HPC possesses periodic macroporous nanostructure (Figure 2c). Figure 2d-e display the TEM images of the N-HPC, showing high uniformity ordered interconnected macropores with the pore size of ${\sim}170$ nm. Figure S4 reveals the microstructure of the polyhedral N-HPC. The wall thickness of the N-HPC is ${\sim}20\mathrm{nm}$ . In addition, as shown in Figure 2f, it is obviously that N-HPC is amorphous carbon according to high-resolution TEM image (HRTEM). The corresponding element mapping images (Figure ${\bf29-j}$ ) confirm the uniform doping of N over the entire N-HPC which attributes to the in-situ N-doped.
The N-HPC and N-PC samples are further explored by the X-ray diffraction (XRD) in Figure 3a. Two broad peaks centered at $\sim25^{\circ}$ and $\sim44^{\circ}$ are, respectively, in agreement with the (002) and (101) planes of graphite. These further confirm the amorphous nature of the N-HPC. And it is similar to the abovementioned HRTEM results. Compared with $\mathrm{D}_{002}({\sim}3.35\mathrm{\AA})$ of natural graphite, the interlayer distance (3.56 Å) of (002) planes, is much larger. And the interlayer distance can be calculated according to the Bragg’s law. The much enhanced interlayer distance is favorable for the rapid diffusion of the oversize alkali metal ions like potassium ions.39 For comparison, the XRD patterns of the 3DOM-ZIF-8 and C-ZIF-8 are display in Figure S5, which well consistent with simulated.40 The Raman spectra exhibits two peaks centered around 1351 and $1594~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ , indexing to the D-band (the defect-cause band) and G-band (in-plane vibrational band) (Figure 3b).41 The intensity rate of D-band and G-band, marked as $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ , could indicate the degree of graphitization. The N-HPC and N-PC samples display the same $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ value of 0.98, indicating the low degree of the graphitization.
Pore size distributions and the specific surface area of the N-HPC sample were measured (Figure 3c). The nitrogen adsorption-desorption isotherms exhibit a type IV isotherms which represent the mesoporous structures.42 In addition, micropores are also demonstrated due to a high nitrogen adsorption at lower pressure. The N-HPC sample displays large specific surface area $(820\ \mathrm{\m}^{2}\ \mathrm{\g}^{-1})$ ) with substantial micropores and mesopores distributed, while the N-PC samples show low specific surface area ( $750\mathrm{m}^{2}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}.$ ) with only micropores distributed (Figure S6). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements are performed to further probe the element constitution and chemical state of N-HPC. The overall XPS figure of N-HPC (Figure 3d) include C, N, and O, with content of $87.52\ \mathrm{at\%}$ , $8.35~\mathrm{at\%}$ and $4.14~\mathrm{at\%}$ , respectively. N element originates from the ligand of 3DOM-ZIF-8, which could realize the homogeneous insitu N-doping in N-HPC. The N 1s spectrum of high-resolution of N-HPC could well fitted into four components, corresponding to pyridinic-N (N-6 at 398.5 eV), pyrrolicN (N-5 at $399.9\mathrm{eV}$ ), quaternary-N (N-Q at $401.1\mathrm{eV}$ ) and N-oxide (N-O at $402.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ ), respectively (Figure 3e). The N-6 is the dominant form of existence. For the C 1s spectra, peaks at 248.8, 285.8, 287.4, and $290.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be associated with the $\displaystyle\mathbf{sp}^{2}$ - hybrizied graphitic carbon, C-N/C-O, $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C=O/C=N}$ , and $scriptstyle{\mathrm{O=C-O}}$ , respectively (Figure 3f).43, 44 According to the previous report, N-5 and N-6 could be served as the reacting active sites and generates more defects in graphene layer, promoting surface-dominated capacitive process for $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ storage.
The embed and extraction behavior of K-ion in N-HPC and the N-PC was examined by galvanostatic charge/discharge cycling and cyclic voltammetry (CV). CV curves of the N-HPC anode within the voltage scope of 0.01-3.0 V (vs. $\mathrm{K^{+}/K}$ ) at a low sweep rate of $0.1\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ were displayed in Figure 4a. In the initial discharge process, the broad peak appears at ${\sim}0.6\mathrm{V}$ and disappears in the succeeding cycles, which could put down to the solid-electrolyte interface (SEI) formed. A intense slop down to 0.01V is due to the K-intercalation in Super P.45 Accordingly, the first anodic scan show broad peak $(0.1\sim0.6\mathrm{V})$ ), suggesting the $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ extraction from N-HPC. In the following cycles, no evident changes could be observed, reveal the excellent electrochemical reversibility of N-HPC. Figure 4b&S7 describe the galvanostatic charge/discharge pattern of NHPC. The large capacity loss at first cycles is noticed indicating the low initial Coulombic efficiency (CE) (Figure S7). Moreover, the high non-reversible capacity can be ascribed to two reasons. Firstly, the large specific surface areas cause surface adsorption and contact with electrolyte forming more SEI film. Secondly, higher Ndoped introduced generates more defect sites in N-HPC resulting in the irreversible processes of depotassiation/potassiation.46The potassiation/depotassiation curves of NHPC, with high capacity remained, are almost overlapped after 50 cycles, displaying the brilliant cyclability. The voltage plateau surpasses the potassium ion deposition potential. It is effectively avoiding the harmful of dendrite formation .23 Different from potassium ions insertion into graphite, the potassiation/depotassiation profile in N-HPC exhibit a slope characteristic. It is almost identical to those previously published in nongraphitic carbon materials. These phenomena can be explained by following two aspects. First, the sloping curve is due to the homogeneous chemical reaction depended on voltage, as the adsorption/desorption in the surface. The abundant defects and tremendous specific surface area in N-HPC can adsorb large amount of $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ , resulting in the monotonic slope. Second, potassium ion, with large size, shows stagnant kinetics in heterogeneous chemical reaction. The cycling property of anodes are further evaluated (Figure 4c). The N-HPC anode exhibits a remarkable specific capacity of 292 mAh $\mathrm{g}^{-}$ 1 after 400 cycles at $0.1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ . In contrast, N-PC only shows 116 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 200 cycles. Moreover, the N-HPC anode shows excellent rate capacity in large range ampere densities $(0.1{-}10.0\mathrm{~A~g^{-}}^{1})$ . Figure 4d&S8 exhibit rate capacity and CE of NHPC electrode at different ampere density. It shows reversible capacities of 345, 280, 223, 180, 149 and $133\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ at the ampere densities of 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0 and 3.0 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively (Figure 4d). Indeed, the ampere densities reach up to 5.0 and 10.0 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , it also delivers 116 and 94 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ considerable capacity, respectively. Notably, N-HPC electrode remains cycling stability during the next 40 cycles at $0.1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , delivering $279\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . In case of the N-PC, it shows lower capacity than those of NHPC at different rates. The capacities fading become more dramatic with raising the current densities. At the rate of $3.0\mathrm{Ag^{-1}}$ , N-PC anode delivers very low capacity of 15 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Figure 4e exhibits ultra-stable cycling life of N-HPC at 2.0 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . It demonstrates the remarkable stability and reversibility with remaining highly capacity of 157 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 12000 cycles and nearly $100\%$ CE. Compared to the recent literature works, our N-HPC show the remarkable reversible capacity at low current density (Figure 5a). What’s more, the long cycle life of N-HPC for KIBs is best than that of carbon-based composite anode (Figure 5b). The ultra-long cycles life and extraordinary rate capability of the N-HPC for KIBs are obtained because of the unique N-doped hierarchical 3D porous structure. The 3D ordered macroporous could maintain the N-HPC electrode material stability and mechanical flexibility, which could alleviate the volume expansion and avoid N-HPC material pulverization during repeated potassiation/depotassiation. In addition, the interconnected microporous and ultrathin walls render improved mass transfer and electrolyte accessibility through the whole system. Importantly, the N-doping in the carbon creates added defects, resulting in improving reactivity and electronic conductivity of carbon matrix.
To investigate the distinction of kinetics between the N-HPC and the N-PC, Galvanostatic intermittent titration (GITT) is conducted to ascertain the diffusion coefficient (D) of K-ions in N-PC and N-HPC during the process of potassiation. The potential response of the electrodes is displayed in Figure S9a during GITT measurement. In relaxation period, the change of voltage presents the overpotential at the each potassiation stage. On the whole, the N-PC displays the higher overpotential than that of N-HPC. According to the curves of GITT, the evolving diffusion coefficients as a function of state of voltage is obtained. The calculation details are shown in Figure S10.The N-HPC shows high diffusion coefficient about 3-5 times higher than that of the N-PC between $0.3\mathrm{~V~}$ and $2.0\mathrm{V}$ (Figure S9b). It indicates that the $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ diffusion is much quicker in N-HPC, attributing to the combination of hierarchical porous structure. In terms of reaction kinetics, the N-HPC shows a lower charge transfer resistance (Figure S11) than that of the N-PC, accounting for a better rate capability of the N-HPC.
The adsorption behaviors of K on macroporous and microporous structures with N doping were investigated by theoretical calculations. As illustrated in Figure 6a-c, the adsorption energies of potassium on the most stable sites on macroporous N-6 and N-5 structures are - $-1.17\mathrm{eV}$ and $-1.34\mathrm{eV}$ . The adsorption energy on microporous 3NV structure is $-2.15\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which is much stronger than that on mcroporous structure. To evaluate the diffusion ability of potassium on macroporous and microporous structures, the adsorption energy differences between the most stable sites and their corresponding sites are also calculated as shown in Figure 6d-f.47 The calculated results are $0.41\ \mathrm{eV}$ , $0.34\mathrm{eV}$ and $1.31\mathrm{eV}$ for N-6, N-5 and 3NV structures, respectively, which indicate that the potential surface of potassium on macroporous structure is much flatter than that on microporous structure. Thus, the diffusion of potassium in macroporous N-HPC is expected to be faster than that on microporous structure. It is noteworthy that the adsorption energy of K on N-doped graphene at each investigated site is stronger than that on graphene without N-doping $\left(-0.60\ \mathrm{eV}\right)$ , which indicates that the doping of nitrogen enhances the ability of K storage(Figure S12).45
To explore the mechanism of potassium ion storage, electrochemical kinetics tests were employed by using CV measurement at varying sweep rates ${0.3}\sim4.0\mathrm{mV}$ $\mathbf{s}^{\scriptscriptstyle-1}$ ). A curves shape of CV of N-HPC are maintained consistently with the increasing scan rates within the sweep rate of the test, being in contrast to N-PC, whose peak shows large polarization (Figure 7a-b). The result indicates better reserving of surfacedominated characteristic enabled by large exposed surface. To further evaluate the reaction mechanisms, the level of capacitive effect is probed by drawing the log (sweep rate) − log (peak current) curve ground on Formula: $i=a\nu^{b}$ , which $i$ stand for peak current, $\nu$ stand for scan rate, a and b represent constants depended on the intercept and the slope of the log (sweep rate)−log (peak current) plot. Generally, the slope value approaches 1.0 signifying capacitive response, while 0.5 indicating the diffusioncontrolled intercalation reaction. The plot applied on the depotassiation peak current is displayed in Figure 7c. A well linear relationship can be observed about N-HPC and N-PC, which the slope values are fitted to 0.95 and 0.73, respectively. Therefore, fast kinetics of N-HPC is dominated by a surface reaction, whereas low b value of N-PC indicates the hybrid reaction mechanisms of capacitive process and diffusion-controlled reaction.
The current response $(i)$ of measuring at the fixed voltage eV is able to distinguish into the two sections of the contribution by the rule: $i\ \mathrm{(V)}=k_{1}\nu+k_{2}\nu^{1/2}$ , which $k_{1}\nu$ represents the contribution of diffusion-controlled process, $k_{2}\nu^{1/2}$ correspond to the surface-dependent process. Figure S14 shows the typical CV profile (cyan region) at $4.0\:\mathrm{mV\:s^{-1}}$ and fitting capacitive curve (red region). A surface-dominated contribution occupies the proportional of $79\%$ for N-HPC, considerably higher than $47\%$ of N-PC sample. The capacitive contributions at different scan rates are obtained by using similar analysis (Figure 7d). The proportional of capacitive enlarges with the increase of scanning rate for both N-HPC and N-PC. The N-HPC possesses higher fraction at all sweep rates, which also proves the ordered macropores exposed more external flaw that could boost potassium adsorption and accelerate the kinetics process.
In-situ Raman spectra were applied to further investigate the N-HPC properties during the process of potassiation and depotassiation. Schematic illustration for in-situ Raman spectra measurements are demonstrated in Figure S15. Figure S16 display the cyclic voltammetry of N-HPC during in-situ Raman spectroscopy testing at sweep rate of $0.2~\mathrm{mV~s^{-1}}$ . Figure 8a-b show the Raman spectroscopy at opted voltage according to the CV of Figure S16. During the potassiation process (Figure 8a), three phenomena were obviously observed. Firstly, the G peak gradually moved from $1595\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ to 1558 $\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ when the voltages from 3.01 V to $0.3\mathrm{~V~}$ . Secondly, there was no obvious symmetrical G peak at 0.01 V. Thirdly, the $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ values are become decreased. These phenomena can be explained with the following reasons. With the potassium embedded, the steric hindrance and electron density are increased that bring about stretching of the in-plane C-C bonding. This expansion generates biaxial strain causing the G peak red shift.48, 49 The G peak signal becomes very weak at $0.01\mathrm{V}$ due to interference between the metallic like behavior of intercalation compounds and the resonant phonon scattering process. The decrease of $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ value indicates the increase of graphitization degree during potassiation.23 Figure 8b shows the Raman spectra at depotassiation process. As the voltage from $0.01\mathrm{V}$ increased to $3.00\mathrm{V}$ , the G peak is gradually blue shifted and the $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ value is also increased. Finally, the G peak and the $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ value restore to the original states. Based on the above in-situ Raman spectra analysis, $I n$ -situ Raman spectra reveals the highly reversible processes during the (de)potassiation, we speculate the K-ion storage mechanism of the N-HPC in Figure S17. Potassium ions absorb onto the surface active sites and nanopore filling are analogous to adsorption.
In conclusion, N-doped hierarchical porous carbon was obtained by carbonization 3D ordered macroporous (3DOM) ZIF-8. Owing to the reduced diffusion distance of the 3D bicontinuous porous frameworks and boosted electronic conductivity of the nitrogen doped carbon, the N-HPC shows excellent electrochemical properties and ultra-long cycle stability for KIBs. The N-HPC exhibits a higher capacity of $292~\mathrm{mAh}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $0.1\mathrm{Ag^{-1}}$ , superb rate capacity of 94 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $10.0\mathrm{Ag^{-1}}$ and long cycle life (157 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ for 12000 cycles at $2.0\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}\$ . The GITT testing and the first-principles calculations reveal the fast diffusion of $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ in N-HPC. Furthermore, In-situ Raman spectroscopy testing reveals the highly reversible processes of potassiation and depotassiation. Thus, 3D ordered MOF-derived materials bring new perspectives in the field of 3D ordered macroporous materials for other applications (i.e. energy conversion, photocatalysis and electrocatalysis).
